```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: red
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
- The reported "over-count" is a tautological identity, not an empirical finding: by construction split_rate == (recall_overlap@k − completeness_whole@k) WHENEVER the retriever ranks an overlapping fragment chunk in top-k AND no whole-span chunk exists. With recall@k pinned at 1.000 in every cell, the experiment is mechanically re-deriving its own definition (P[overlap-hit ∧ ¬whole-hit] = P[split] conditional on retrievability). No new fact is established beyond "if you split a span, no chunk contains the whole span" — which is true a priori.
- The split-rate scaling with span_len / chunk_size / overlap is elementary combinatorics of a sliding window over a uniform insertion position: P(split) ≈ max(0, (span_len − overlap − 1) / (chunk_size − overlap)) to first order. The cited numbers (e.g. chunk=32/ov=0/span=32 → 0.965; chunk=64/ov=0/span=16 → 0.215; chunk=64/ov=16/span=32 → 0.275) match this closed form to within sampling noise. This is "expected complexity," not an emergent phenomenon.
- The "score-distortion" magnitude (recall 1.000 vs completeness 0.785/0.544/0.035) is fully explained by caveat #1: gold tokens are unique/distinctive, so the lexical retriever cannot fail to rank a fragment in top-1. The dramatic gap is a sim artifact of the always-retrievable-fragment assumption, not a property of recall@k.
CONFOUNDS:
- recall@k=1.000 EVERY cell ⇒ the metric being "compared against" is degenerate; the contrast collapses to (1 − completeness@k), which is just the split rate. The retriever variable is held constant at a corner case where it carries no information.
- k-invariance is presented as data; it is forced by the unique-gold-token construction. Any "k matters" finding is precluded by design — yet the claim is about a metric "standardly reported at k."
- Query construction uses a subset of gold tokens — guaranteeing fragment chunks score high. This is the mechanism, not a discovery.
- Synthetic spans inserted at controlled positions with uniform insertion ⇒ split rate is a deterministic function of (span_len, chunk_size, overlap), not an empirical distribution over a real corpus.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
- That a real dense retriever (or even BM25 on a real corpus with non-unique answer tokens) would still place a fragment chunk in top-k at near-100% rate. Caveat #1 admits this is likely FALSE, which would shrink the headline numbers materially and could invert the sign of the "score distortion" (a real retriever may MISS the fragment, lowering recall@k toward completeness@k).
- That answer-completeness@k is NOT what practitioners report. The packet asserts this as folklore but cites no survey of RAG eval practice. The framing question (ii) from the orchestrator is left unresolved: containment/exact-containment/answer-in-context metrics ARE standard in QA-RAG eval (e.g. "answer recall," "context precision/recall" in RAGAS, "has-answer" in DPR/NQ evals). The "unreported in practice" premise is unsupported.
- That a fragment chunk is "insufficient to answer." Not measured — no reader, no EM/F1. The whole downstream-cost question (the only thing that would convert this from definitional to consequential) is deferred to L1 and is currently unevidenced.
- That gold spans in real QA corpora are 15–40+ tokens "frequently" exceeding typical overlaps. Asserted, not measured (NQ short answers are typically 1–5 tokens; long-answer spans are paragraph-scale where the framing changes entirely).
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
- Only 8 seeds × 200 docs/cell. CIs reported as "lo=" only (one-sided?). No across-seed variance decomposition; no test that the over-count != split_rate (the interesting falsification, since equality = tautology).
- No comparison to the closed-form combinatorial prediction. If split_rate matches the analytic formula within CI, that is direct evidence the result is theory-predicted, not empirical.
- k=1 "tiny over-count (~0.005–0.05) even at overlap≥span" attributed to score ties is hand-waved; no tie-breaking audit, no sensitivity to IDF normalization.
- No power analysis for the "materially exceeds 20%" threshold; the 20% bar is post-hoc-friendly (some cells pass, some fail, scope is sliced to the passing ones).
RATIONALE: The L0 design makes the "discovery" a definitional identity: with recall@k pinned at 1.0 by construction (unique gold tokens + lexical retriever), recall@k − completeness@k IS the split rate, and the split rate is a closed-form function of (span_len, chunk_size, overlap). Caveat #1 in the packet concedes the load-bearing assumption ("the fragment is retrievable") is sim-artifactual and that a real dense retriever may close the gap. The "score distortion" headline (96.5 pts at chunk32/span32) is the worst case of this artifact, not evidence about real RAG eval. On framing (i): recall@k IS standard, but on framing (ii) the orchestrator's exact concern lands — answer-containment/has-answer metrics ARE in widespread practitioner use (RAGAS context-recall, DPR has-answer, NQ short-answer match), so "the over-count is unreported" is asserted-not-shown folklore. The only contribution that would survive — downstream EM/F1 cost of recall@k-selected vs completeness@k-selected chunks — is explicitly deferred. As-is, this is a measurement of a tautology under a corner-case retriever, with the magnitude admittedly inflated and the "novelty vs folklore" question unresolved. Red: do not promote to L1 without (a) breaking the recall@k=1.0 degeneracy with a real retriever on a real corpus, (b) showing the empirical split rate diverges from the combinatorial prediction (or admitting it doesn't and reframing as "here is the closed form"), and (c) a literature pass on RAGAS/DPR/NQ metrics to establish that whole-span completeness is genuinely unreported.
```
